(6561872, 973636, 30982, '<td valign="top">An OK interpretation of the challenge - I do like the idea of the two bottles, one glass - makes for a pleasing arrangement.  But if you''re going to shoot glass, it must be spotless - no dust, no marks, no flaws in the glass.  I like the "barn door" reflection - you''ll note a lot of advertising with wine bottles has this reflection - gives definition and shape, I think.  But if you REALLY want to shoot glass well, go spend some time in <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=43774" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">karennfld</a>''s portfolio.  Read the notes she has on her glassware shots to see how she does the lighting.  Note that all the glassware is pristine (and in non-basic challenges you can clean up some of it in post production.)</td>', 'An OK interpretation of the challenge - I do like the idea of the two bottles, one glass - makes for a pleasing arrangement.  But if you''re going to shoot glass, it must be spotless - no dust, no marks, no flaws in the glass.  I like the "barn door" reflection - you''ll note a lot of advertising with wine bottles has this reflection - gives definition and shape, I think.  But if you REALLY want to shoot glass well, go spend some time in  karennfld''s portfolio.  Read the notes she has on her glassware shots to see how she does the lighting.  Note that all the glassware is pristine (and in non-basic challenges you can clean up some of it in post production.)', '2011-10-05 22:02:38.000000', NULL, 0),
